  "reason": "The records collectively disclose the essential processing strategy and strengthening logic. Multiple refractory-HEA studies use elemental-powder mechanical alloying under argon followed by vacuum SPS, while R3 directly links longer milling to deformation, grain refinement, Hall–Petch hardening, and hardness near 676 HV. R9 supplies the combined grain-boundary and lattice-strain/solid-solution strengthening explanation, R10 establishes strong nanocrystalline grain-boundary hardening, and R19 shows milling-driven solid-solution and phase evolution. R4 is especially relevant compositionally because severe deformation of AlCoCrFeNi produces defect-rich refined material and changes the FCC/BCC balance. Other records explain atomic-radius-driven lattice distortion and solid-solution strengthening and identify contamination risks favoring relatively short milling. Thus an expert could plausibly propose short HEBM plus SPS for CoCrFeNiTi and predict a strong nanocrystalline mixed-phase bulk, although the precise 83% FCC/17% BCC ratio, Ti-specific phase-stabilization rationale, processing window, and exact strength values are not directly disclosed."
